# React Frontend Conventions (Scholar)

## Component Patterns

- Use functional components exclusively (no class components)
- One component per file; filename matches component name in PascalCase
- Keep components focused — extract sub-components when a file exceeds ~200 lines
- Use composition over prop drilling; leverage Context API for cross-cutting concerns

```jsx
function PaperCard({ paper, onSelect }) {
  // Guard clauses first
  if (!paper) return null;

  return (
    <div className="paper-card" onClick={() => onSelect(paper.id)}>
      <h3>{paper.title}</h3>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Directory Structure

```
src/
  components/       # Reusable UI components
    styles/         # Component CSS files (ComponentName.css)
  pages/            # Route-level page components
    styles/         # Page CSS files
    features/       # Feature-specific page components
  services/         # API service modules (*.api.ts)
  hooks/            # Custom React hooks (useXxx.js/ts)
  contexts/         # React Context providers
  stores/           # State management
  utils/            # Utility functions
  assets/           # Static assets (images, fonts)
  admin/            # Admin dashboard pages and components
```

## Hooks Best Practices

- Custom hooks start with `use` prefix: `useSearch`, `usePagination`
- Keep hooks focused on a single concern
- Use `useCallback` for event handlers passed to child components
- Use `useMemo` for expensive computations only when profiling shows a need
- Always specify complete dependency arrays in `useEffect`
- Clean up side effects in `useEffect` return functions

## API Service Layer

API calls go through service files in `src/services/`:

```typescript
// services/scholar.api.ts
import { client } from './client';

export const searchPapers = (query: string, limit: number) =>
  client.get('/api/v1/scholar/papers/search', { params: { query, limit } });
```

- Use the shared `client` instance (Axios with interceptors)
- Group API calls by domain module
- Handle auth token injection via Axios interceptors (already configured)
- Never use `fetch()` directly; always use the shared Axios client

## State Management

- **Local state**: `useState` for component-scoped state
- **Complex state**: `useReducer` for state with multiple sub-values
- **Shared state**: React Context API via `src/contexts/`
- **Server state**: React Query via `src/hooks/queries/`
- Auth state managed by AuthContext (token in localStorage)

## CSS Conventions

- One CSS file per component, placed in adjacent `styles/` directory
- CSS class names use kebab-case: `.paper-card`, `.search-input`
- No inline styles unless dynamically computed
- Use CSS custom properties (variables) for theme values
- Mobile-first responsive design with breakpoints: 640px, 768px, 1024px

## Error Handling

- Use `ErrorBoundary` component for catching render errors
- Show user-friendly error messages (never raw error objects)
- Handle loading/error/empty states in every data-fetching component
- Use try-catch in async functions; display errors via toast or inline messages

## Performance

- Lazy-load route-level components with `React.lazy` + `Suspense`
- Use `React.memo` only for components that re-render with unchanged props
- Virtualize long lists with `useVirtualList` hook
- Optimize images: use WebP format, set explicit width/height

## Admin Dashboard

- Admin routes live under `src/admin/` and render at `/admin/*`
- Admin components follow the same patterns as user-facing components
- Use Recharts for admin analytics visualizations
